WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:06.160
Take the Twilight movies and mash ‘em up
with Nine And A Half Weeks, and add a hint

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.620
of Secretary. And what do you get? That’s
right, Fifty Shades Of Grey!

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.599
Yes, British author EL James’s best-selling
erotic novel has been given the big-screen

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:19.220
treatment, and the result is… to be expected.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.570
First off, let’s talk about the leads.
Dakota Johnson, who you’ll have seen in

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.590
movies like 21 Jump Street and The Social
Network, plays Anastasia Steele.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:32.369
Ana’s a lip-biting college student who does
her sick friend a favour by stepping in at

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.710
the last minute to interview billionaire businessman
Christian Grey for the college newspaper.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:43.370
Ana is pretty much just another weak, female
character, though on a few occasions she shows

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.619
herself capable of taking more control than
expected.

00:00:46.619 --> 00:00:50.369
This is a tough role for an actress, not least
because of the nudity.

00:00:50.369 --> 00:00:55.119
But Johnson is one of the movie’s main strengths
– she delivers a very natural performance

00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:59.530
as Ana despite the fifty shades of shit script
she has to swim through to get there.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:04.600
I really hope this movie launches her on to
much, much better things, as she’s got a

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:05.570
lot of talent.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.000
Jamie Dornan stars as the mega-rich Grey,
who’s big on control, shall we say.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.430
Dornan, who was so great as the soft-spoken
serial killer in the BBC series The Fall,

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:19.630
which there’s actually a little allusion
to in this movie, does a good job as Grey,

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:23.780
despite the fact he seems to get the lion’s
share of laughable lines.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.450
As for the dialogue, it is excruciatingly
bad.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.310
I’m guessing that’s because the screenwriter
was hamstrung by the incredible level of control

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:37.520
that author EL James exercised over the movie,
which is a very unusual situation, especially

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.100
for someone with so little experience.
There are countless cornball lines, where

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.920
you just think to yourself, ‘no one would
say that in real life!’

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.030
At least, it provides plenty of laughs, even

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.140
if many of them are entirely unintentional.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:59.140
As for the story, well, that essentially follows
Ana’s initiation by Grey into his world

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.820
of BDSM – that’s bondage, discipline,
domination, submission, and sado-masochism,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.220
just in case you didn’t know.
In some ways, it’s hard to believe Fifty

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:11.370
Shades Of Grey started life as Twilight fan
fiction, but all that stuff where Grey tells

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.690
Ana she should stay away from him certainly
reminds me of Edward and Bella in Twilight,

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.130
and there’s Ana’s male friend who wants
much more than just friendship too.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:25.450
The plot of Fifty Shades brings with it an
array of clichés, including the young, unsophisticated

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.480
woman with no money who falls for the rich,
sophisticated guy, who in this case happens

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.110
to be only slightly older.
Yes, poor Cinderella gets her rich prince,

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:39.030
but not without a good spanking first.
Then there’s the cliché of the woman who

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.340
wants a bad boy, or at least an emotionally
damaged one, that they can make good.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:49.180
But there’s also a weird, creepy, horror-vibe
going on with Grey basically stalking Ana,

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:53.849
even turning up in her home, uninvited and
unannounced.

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:59.000
It reminded me a bit of the terrible rom-com
This Means War, which thought for some unknown

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.390
reason that it’d be romantic and funny to
have two male friends, who were after the

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:08.270
same woman, turning up randomly in her home
and tracking her every movement.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:13.430
Oh, and after how we’re introduced to Grey,
the scene where he hands Ana his business

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:17.739
card, and the camera lingers over it, made
me half expect him to launch into a discussion

00:03:17.739 --> 00:03:20.709
of its colouring and lettering, à la American
Psycho.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:23.890
The start of the movie all feels like it happens
rather quickly.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.569
One minute, Ana and Grey are meeting for the
first time, and next minute they’re hot

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:30.660
as hell for each other.
Yet, it never felt like there was any real

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.190
heat between the leads in their meet-cute.
But while the development of their desire

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:40.709
did feel rushed, the film overall felt long.
I think it was the ridiculous dialogue, which

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:45.110
did make me laugh, but just got more and more
tedious as the film went on.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:49.720
Obviously, there’s a lot of nudity, but
given what I understand about the source material,

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.530
the adaptation feels rather tame, pretty soft-core
in comparison.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:57.440
It’s basically Nine And A Half Weeks with
added male nudity.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.300
Director Sam Taylor-Johnson was always going
to be waging an up-hill battle to make even

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.120
a polyester purse, never mind a silk one,
out of a sow’s ear.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:10.690
But she’s obviously tried her best, especially
given the on-set battles between herself and

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:15.140
author EL James that she’s spoken about.
As for the look of the movie, cinematographer

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:19.459
Seamus McGarvey’s worked his magic yet again.
You’ll have seen McGarvey’s work on movies

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.460
like Atonement, Avengers Assemble, and We
Need To Talk About Kevin.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:27.240
And in Fifty Shades Of Grey he’s given us
another astonishingly well-shot movie.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.509
The movie’s design team, David and Sandy
Wasco, and its costume designer Mark Bridges

00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:35.020
have also all done a great job creating Ana
and Grey’s worlds through their apartments

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:35.909
and clothes.

00:04:35.909 --> 00:04:42.509
So, to sum up: Fifty Shades Of Grey is a yawnsome
movie riddled with clichés and some absolutely

00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:47.150
awful dialogue.
But Dakota Johnson’s performance and Seamus

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:51.289
McGarvey’s beautiful cinematography help
it rise above what it is.

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:55.050
Fifty Shades Of Grey gets 2 out of 5 Flicks.

